{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":2535789,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]],"date-time":"2026-10-27T15:03:24Z","timestamp":1793113404924,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":27,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"12","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,5]],"date-time":"2024-11-05T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1730764800000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["ascpt.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["CPT Pharmacom &amp; Syst Pharma"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Sensitivity analyses are important components of physiologically based pharmacokinetic (PBPK) model development and are required by regulatory agencies for PBPK submissions. They assess the impact of parametric uncertainty and variability on model estimates, aid model optimization by identifying parameters requiring calibration, and enable the testing of assumptions within PBPK models. One\u2010at\u2010a\u2010time (OAT) sensitivity analyses quantify the impact on a model output in response to changes in a single parameter while holding others fixed. Global sensitivity analysis (GSA) methods provide more comprehensive assessments by accounting for changes in all uncertain or variable parameters, though at a higher computational cost. This tutorial article presents a software package for conducting both OAT and GSA of PBPK models built in the Open Systems Pharmacology (OSP) Suite. The tool is accessible through either an R script or a graphical user interface, and the outputs consist of sensitivity metrics of pharmacokinetic (PK) parameters, such as\n                    <jats:italic>C<\/jats:italic>\n                    <jats:sub>max<\/jats:sub>\n                    and AUC, evaluated with respect to model input parameters. Results are formatted according to regulatory standards. The OAT analysis methods comprise two\u2010way local sensitivity analyses and probabilistic uncertainty analyses, whereas the GSA methods include the Morris, Sobol, and EFAST methods. These analyses can be conducted on single PBPK models or pairs of models for the evaluation of the sensitivity of PK parameter ratios\u00a0in drug\u2013drug interaction studies. The practical application of the package is demonstrated through three illustrative case studies.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/psp4.13256","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,5]],"date-time":"2024-11-05T06:45:52Z","timestamp":1730789152000},"page":"2052-2067","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":22,"title":["Global sensitivity analysis of Open Systems Pharmacology Suite physiologically based pharmacokinetic models"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"13","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4620-9830","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abdulkarim","family":"Najjar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beiersdorf AG  Hamburg Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9419-1797","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abdullah","family":"Hamadeh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Pharmacy University of Waterloo  Kitchener Ontario Canada"},{"name":"Systems In Silico Ltd.  Waterloo Ontario Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sophia","family":"Krause","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beiersdorf AG  Hamburg Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andreas","family":"Schepky","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beiersdorf AG  Hamburg Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andrea",